      ":question/problem": "Evaluate ${\\int}_{C}F\\cdot\\text{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=(- 2 y)\\,i+(2 x)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(\\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j$ for $t\\in[0,\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}].$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\text{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\quad y=\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(- 2 y)\\,i+(2 x)\\,j \\\\  & =(- 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j \\\\  & =(- 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j \\\\  & =(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =((- 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j)\\cdot((\\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j) \\\\  & =(- 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\cdot(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)})+(2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\cdot(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}) \\\\  & =(- 2 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)})+(- 2 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\text{d}r & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}(-2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\left[- 2 t\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}} \\\\  & =((- 3 \\pi))-((0)) \\\\  & =(- 3 \\pi)-(0) \\\\  & =- 3 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$",
